  "worked_solution": "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t) & ={y}^{\u2032} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t) & ={y}^{\u2033}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}={y}^{\u2033}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{\u2033}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}={t}^{2}{y}^{\u2032}+t+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}={t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}={t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)={t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1.\n$$",
